00:01:40.369 --> 00:01:47.280
Speaker 1: And of course, it continues to&nbsp;
be stunning that not only can Trump run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:52.560
for president, he is running for president,&nbsp;
even though the campaign so far has been a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:57.240
complete and total flop. Now, let's be very real&nbsp;
about what I know many of you consider to be the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:02.940
most important question here. Do presidents get&nbsp;
indicted in the United States? Could a president&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:07.680
get indicted? Why has a president never been&nbsp;
indicted in the United States? Let's start the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:12.120
conversation with a reminder that no president&nbsp;
has ever done the things Donald Trump did. Put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:17.160
that aside for now, because we want to talk now&nbsp;
about historical precedent. To put it in one word,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:24.300
tradition is the reason that presidents have not&nbsp;
historically been indicted by the Department of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:32.100
Justice. And what I mean by that is historically&nbsp;
out of the principle of executive immunity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:39.420
which holds the president is not subject to&nbsp;
criminal prosecution while serving in office.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:46.800
The DOJ has not pursued charges against a&nbsp;
president. And the principle behind that is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:55.080
if you charge a sitting president, you inhibit&nbsp;
their ability to do the job of being president of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.080 --> 00:03:02.760
the United States. And if a president worries that&nbsp;
what they do might get them indicted while they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:10.200
are president, they might act other than how they&nbsp;
believe is in the best interest of the country for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:16.080
fear of a personal prosecution or because of&nbsp;
the distraction of a criminal trial. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:22.860
after leaving office, there is nothing that&nbsp;
would prevent or preclude a president from being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:30.540
investigated, from being potentially charged with&nbsp;
crimes and from being prosecuted. Importantly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:36.000
you also have impeachment. Impeachment for&nbsp;
high crimes and misdemeanors is a completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:41.400
separate process from criminal prosecution and&nbsp;
doesn't require any crime to have been committed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:45.960
Important to understand that the January&nbsp;
six committee investigation and potential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:50.700
criminal referrals would not be for impeachable&nbsp;
offenses because the House could handle that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:54.780
itself. Trump's out of office. He's already&nbsp;
been impeached twice. This is about in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.780 --> 00:04:00.420
investigative carrying out of their investigative&nbsp;
role. The House may come across what are apparent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:04.860
crimes and refer those to the Department&nbsp;
of Justice. Tradition is the only reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:10.860
why historically you have not seen prosecution&nbsp;
of a President Trump is now out of office. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:16.500
may know within hours whether the House committee&nbsp;
says Trump's on the list of people who committed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:21.660
